QUESTIONS83=[
 'For A(2,2), B(3,1), claimed chord endpoints of x²+y²−4x−2y+4 = 0, show (a) the perpendicular from the center bisects AB; (b) the center-to-midpoint line is perpendicular to AB.',
 'For A(0,0), B(0,3), claimed chord endpoints of x²+y²+4x−5y = 0, show (a) the perpendicular from the center bisects AB; (b) the center-to-midpoint line is perpendicular to AB.',
 'Show AB and DE are equidistant from C(0,0): (a) x²+y² = 4 with A(−2,0), B(0,2), D(0,2), E(2,0); (b) x²+y² = 16 with A(−4,0), B(0,4), D(0,4), E(4,0).',
 'Show the central angle subtended by minor arc AB is twice the inscribed angle on the major arc: (a) x²+y² = 9 with A(2,√5), B(2,−√5); (b) x²+y² = 4 with A(1,√3), B(1,−√3).',
 'Show the angle in the semicircle of (x−h)²+y² = a² is a right angle: (a) h = 1, a = 2; (b) h = 3, a = 4. In each case the diameter is AB.',
 'Show the perpendicular at the outer endpoint of the radial segment is tangent to the circle: (a) P(1,5), x²+y²+x−5y−2 = 0; (b) P(5,6), x²+y²−22x−4y+25 = 0.'
]
STEPS83=[
 ['Complete squares: (x−2)²+(y−1)² = 1, giving center C(2,1).','A(2,2) and B(3,1) each have squared center-distance 1, so they are valid chord endpoints.','Their midpoint is D(5/2,3/2), and the chord direction B−A = (1,−1).','The center-to-midpoint direction D−C = (1/2,1/2) has dot product zero with the chord direction.','Thus CD is perpendicular to AB and meets it at its midpoint, proving both parts.','The chord is x+y−4 = 0 and the perpendicular bisector is x−y−1 = 0. Their intersection is D, and C lies on the bisector.'],
 ['Check the endpoint condition before applying a chord theorem. At A(0,0) the circle polynomial is 0, but at B(0,3) it is 9−15 = −6.','B is not on the stated circle, so AB is not its chord. Complete squares to find the center C(−2,5/2).','The midpoint of the stated segment AB is D(0,3/2); the center-to-midpoint direction is D−C = (2,−1).','Its dot product with the segment direction (0,3) is −3 ≠ 0, so the requested perpendicularity fails as printed.','The perpendicular from C to the vertical line x = 0 meets it at (0,5/2), not D. Thus it does not bisect the stated segment.','The actual circle intersections with x = 0 solve y²−5y = 0, giving (0,0) and (0,5). Their chord is bisected at (0,5/2), consistent with the theorem. This is a separate explanation, not a replacement of the source point.'],
 ['(a) The chord AB through (−2,0),(0,2) is x−y+2 = 0; DE through (0,2),(2,0) is x+y−2 = 0.','Each origin-to-line distance is 2/√2 = √2, proving equidistance.','Their perpendicular feet are (−1,1) and (1,1), which are their midpoints. Each chord has length 2√2.','(b) The corresponding chord lines are x−y+4 = 0 and x+y−4 = 0.','Each origin-to-line distance is 4/√2 = 2√2. Their midpoint feet are (−2,2) and (2,2); each chord length is 4√2.','All source endpoints satisfy their circle equations. In each case D and B name the same shared endpoint; this does not invalidate the two distinct chords.'],
 ['Choose a convenient point S on the major arc; the inscribed angle is ∠ASB, while the central minor angle is ∠AOB.','(a) Choose S(−3,0). Rays SA = (5,√5), SB = (5,−√5) have dot product 20 and cross magnitude 10√5.','Thus ∠ASB = atan(√5/2) ≈ 48.189685°. The center rays have dot product −1 and cross magnitude 4√5, giving the obtuse ∠AOB ≈ 96.379370°, exactly twice the inscribed angle.','(b) Choose S(−2,0). Rays SA = (3,√3), SB = (3,−√3) have dot product 6 and cross magnitude 6√3.','Thus ∠ASB = 60°. The central rays have dot product −2 and cross magnitude 2√3, so ∠AOB = 120° = 2·60°.','The inscribed-angle theorem extends the equality to every non-endpoint vertex on the major arc. Using ray dot/cross values fixes the obtuse central branches that finite-slope arctangents alone can miss.'],
 ['For a circle centered at (h,0), the horizontal diameter endpoints are A(h−a,0) and B(h+a,0).','At a non-endpoint P(x,y) on the circle, the ray dot product is (A−P)·(B−P) = (x−h)²+y²−a².','The circle equation makes this zero, and both rays are nonzero, so the angle is 90°.','(a) h = 1, a = 2 gives endpoints (−1,0),(3,0) and circle (x−1)²+y² = 4.','(b) h = 3, a = 4 gives endpoints (−1,0),(7,0) and circle (x−3)²+y² = 16.','The proof holds on the upper and lower open semicircles. The diameter endpoints satisfy the circle equation but are excluded because the angle there is undefined.'],
 ['(a) Verify P(1,5) is on the circle: 1+25+1−25−2 = 0. Its center is C(−1/2,5/2) and radius vector to P is (3/2,5/2).','The perpendicular through P has equation 3(x−1)+5(y−5) = 0, or 3x+5y−28 = 0.','Its tangent direction (5,−3) has dot product zero with the radius. The center-to-line distance squared is (17²)/34 = 17/2, equal to the circle’s squared radius.','(b) At P(5,6), the source polynomial is 25+36−110−24+25 = −48, so P is not on the given circle.','Complete squares: (x−11)²+(y−2)² = 100. The point’s squared center-distance is (−6)²+4² = 52 < 100, so it is inside and has no real tangent through it.','The perpendicular through P to its center-to-point vector is 3x−2y−3 = 0, but its center-distance squared is 26²/13 = 52, not 100. It is a secant, not a tangent. Thus (b) cannot be proved as printed.']
]
ANSWERS83=['Perpendicular bisector x−y−1 = 0; midpoint (5/2,3/2).','Invalid chord as printed: B(0,3) is off the circle, and the stated midpoint-perpendicular claims fail.','(a) both distances √2; (b) both distances 2√2.','(a) central ≈ 96.379370°, inscribed ≈ 48.189685°; (b) central 120°, inscribed 60°.','Both angles are 90° for non-endpoint circumference points.','(a) tangent 3x+5y−28 = 0; (b) invalid as printed: P(5,6) is inside the circle.']
REVIEW=[
 ('i','If radius of a circle is 2cm then its equation will be:',['x²+y² = 2','x²+y² = √2','x²+y² = 8','x²+y² = 4'],'d, assuming center at the origin',['A radius alone does not specify a unique circle without a center.','For an arbitrary center (h,k), the equation is (x−h)²+(y−k)² = 4.','All listed choices implicitly use the origin as center.','Under that convention, squaring radius 2 gives 4.','Thus the intended option is (d), with the missing center assumption stated.']),
 ('ii','In the general equation of circle the coordinates of centre are:',['(x,y)','(−x,−y)','(f,g)','(−f,−g)'],'No generally correct printed option; center (−g,−f)',['For x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0, complete squares.','This gives (x+g)²+(y+f)² = g²+f²−c.','The x coordinate of the center is −g and the y coordinate is −f.','Thus the ordered center pair is (−g,−f).','Option (d) swaps the coordinates, and the other options are also not the general center. None is generally correct.']),
 ('iii','For the general equation of the circle the radius can be calculated by r =',[r'\(\sqrt{x^2+y^2-c^2}\)',r'\(\sqrt{(-x)^2+(-y)^2-c^2}\)',r'\(\sqrt{(-g)^2+(-f)^2-c^2}\)',r'\(\sqrt{(-g)^2+(-f)^2+c^2}\)'],'No generally correct printed option; r = √(g²+f²−c)',['Complete squares to obtain (x+g)²+(y+f)² = g²+f²−c.','The squared radius is g²+f²−c, where c is the original constant, not its square.','For a real nondegenerate circle require that value to be positive.','The radius is the nonnegative square root √(g²+f²−c).','Every printed choice uses c² or unrelated point coordinates. None is generally correct.']),
 ('iv','A point of the circle at which a tangent meets the circle is called:',['point of contact','normal','center point','none of these'],'a',['A tangent meets a genuine circle at one point.','That shared circumference point is its contact point.','A normal is a perpendicular line through the contact and center.','The center is inside the circle, not its tangent contact.','Select (a).']),
 ('v',r'If the discriminant of \(m^2(x_1^2-a_2)-2mx_1y_1+(y_1^2-a_2)=0\) is greater than zero then tangent are:',['real and different','imaginary','real and coincident','equal'],'a, under the nondegenerate quadratic interpretation',['A real quadratic with nonzero leading coefficient has two distinct real roots when its discriminant is positive.','Here the roots represent finite tangent slopes under the source’s intended circle setup.','Thus the intended classification is real and different, option (a).','The usual radius-squared symbol is a²; the printed a₂ notation is retained in the question.','When the slope equation drops degree, a vertical tangent needs separate handling; the geometric outside-circle condition still determines two tangent lines.']),
 ('vi','The length of the tangent drawn from P(3,4) on x²+y²−9 = 0 is:',['1','2','3','4'],'d',['The radius is 3 and center is the origin.','The point’s squared center-distance is 3²+4² = 25.','Subtract the squared radius: tangent length squared is 25−9 = 16.','Take the nonnegative square root: length 4.','Select (d).']),
 ('vii','The angle subtended can be calculated by using the trigonometric identity:',[r'\(\tan\theta=(m_3-m_1)/(1+m_1m_2)\)',r'\(\tan2\theta=2\tan\theta/(1-\tan^2\theta)\)',r'\(\tan2\theta=2\tan^2\theta/(1+\tan^2\theta)\)',r'\(\tan2\theta=\tan^2\theta/(1-2\tan^2\theta)\)'],'b',['Use sin2θ = 2sinθcosθ and cos2θ = cos²θ−sin²θ.','Their quotient gives tan2θ = 2sinθcosθ/(cos²θ−sin²θ).','When the divisions are defined, divide numerator and denominator by cos²θ.','This yields 2tanθ/(1−tan²θ), option (b).','The identity gives a tangent value; geometric angle branches still need the actual rays, and undefined-denominator cases must be treated directly.']),
 ('viii','What is the contact point for the line x = 7 touching x²+y²−4x−6y−12 = 0?',['(2,8)','(8,2)','(3,7)','(7,3)'],'d',['Complete squares: (x−2)²+(y−3)² = 25.','The center is (2,3) and radius is 5.','Distance from the center to x = 7 is 5, proving tangency.','The perpendicular projection of the center onto that vertical line is (7,3).','Select (d); substitution gives (7−2)²+(3−3)² = 25.']),
 ('ix','For tangency of y = mx+c to x²+y² = r²:',['c = r√(1+m²)','c = −r√(1+m²)','both option a & b','not option (b) nor option (c)'],'c',['Take r > 0. The origin-to-line distance is |c|/√(1+m²).','Equating it to r gives |c| = r√(1+m²).','Hence both positive and negative c values give tangents.','Options (a) and (b) are the two signs.','Select (c).']),
 ('x','A line perpendicular to the contact point to a circle is called:',['Tangent','Normal','Chord','Diameter'],'b, interpreting “perpendicular to the tangent at the contact point”',['A point alone has no direction, so being perpendicular to a point is not defined.','The intended phrase is a line perpendicular to the tangent at its contact point.','That line is the normal, and it passes through the circle center.','Select (b) under this explicit interpretation.','A chord joins two circumference points; a diameter is a chord through the center, rather than the standard name for the contact normal.'])
]
SUMMARY=[heading('Summary'),paragraph('Standard Form of a Circle: The standard form of a circle with radius r and center C(h,k) is:'),eq(r'(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2'),paragraph('General Form of a Circle: The general form of a circle with radius r = √((−g)²+(−f)²−c) and center C(−g,−f) is:'),eq(r'x^2+y^2+2gx+2fy+c=0'),paragraph('The coefficient of x² is equal to the coefficient of y², and there is no term containing xy and the square of the radius is r² ≥ 0.'),heading('Nature of the circle'),paragraph('If g²+f²−c > 0, the circle is real and different from zero. If g²+f²−c = 0, then the circle shrinks into a point (−g,−f). It is called a point circle. If g²+f²−c < 0, then the circle is imaginary or virtual.'),heading('Condition of Tangency'),paragraph('The condition at which the line y = mx+c should touch the circle x²+y² = a² is:'),eq(r'c=\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}'),paragraph('The equation of any tangent to the circle x²+y² = a² in the slope form is:'),eq(r'y=mx\pm a\sqrt{1+m^2}'),paragraph('The condition at which lx+my+n = 0 should touch x²+y² = a² is:'),eq(r'n=\pm a\sqrt{l^2+m^2}'),paragraph('The condition at which lx+my+n = 0 should touch x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0 is:'),eq(r'(c-f^2)l^2+2fglm+(c-g^2)m^2-2n(gl+fm)+n^2=0'),paragraph('The tangent equation to x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0 at A(x₁,y₁) is:'),eq(r'xx_1+yy_1+g(x+x_1)+f(y+y_1)+c=0'),paragraph('The tangent equation to x²+y² = a² at A(x₁,y₁) is:'),eq(r'xx_1+yy_1=a^2'),paragraph('The normal equation to x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0 at A(x₁,y₁) is:'),eq(r'x(y_1+f)-y(x_1+g)+(gy_1-fx_1)=0'),paragraph('The normal equation to x²+y² = a² at A(x₁,y₁) is:'),eq(r'xy_1-yx_1=0'),paragraph('The length of the tangent drawn from P(x₁,y₁) to x²+y²+2gx+2fy+c = 0 is:'),eq(r'|PT|=\sqrt{x_1^2+y_1^2+2gx_1+2fy_1+c}'),paragraph('The length of the tangent drawn from P(x₁,y₁) to x²+y² = a² is:'),eq(r'|PT|=\sqrt{x_1^2+y_1^2-a^2}'),paragraph('The lengths of the two tangents drawn from P(x₁,y₁) on the given circle are equal.'),note('Use positive radius for a genuine circle, a nonzero normal for a line, and circumference membership for an at-point tangent/normal. Slope forms exclude vertical tangents; implicit forms handle them. External tangent lengths require positive power, with zero as a circumference limit. Negative power means no real tangent. The summary correctly prints l²(c−f²) in the general tangency condition, unlike the extra square in printed 226’s special x+my+n formula. The expanded summary equation equals (n−lg−mf)²−(g²+f²−c)(l²+m²) = 0.')]
